# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  THE DIFFERENTIAL ACCEPT/REJECT PROBE CORPUS
#  (subject: tests/conformance/test_reference_conformance.cpp)
#  D-CONF-REFERENCE-DIFFERENTIAL-ORACLE
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The operator's standing rule, which this corpus exists to MEASURE rather than
# assert: "WE MUST SUPPORT WHAT REFERENCE COMPILERS DO SUPPORT FOR C Language,
# that's it." It is BIDIRECTIONAL, and both directions are defects:
#
#   Direction A — a reference compiler ACCEPTS and DSS REJECTS.
#                 A conformance gap; real corpora fail to build.
#   Direction B — DSS ACCEPTS and NO reference compiler accepts.
#                 An INVENTED extension; code written for DSS builds nowhere else.
#
# Direction B had never been checked before this corpus existed, and the operator
# caught an instance BY EYE (`extern int _fmode "msvcrt.dll";`, probe
# `b_extern_library_realization_string` below). A fix to that one instance is
# worthless; the mechanism is the deliverable.
#
# ── WHAT IS DECLARED HERE AND WHAT IS MEASURED ─────────────────────────────
#
# ★ REFERENCE VERDICTS ARE MEASURED, NEVER TAKEN FROM THIS FILE. Every verdict
#   in the census comes from a compiler this harness located on THIS host and ran
#   on THIS probe, and is reported with that compiler's identity and the exact
#   `-std=` / `/std:` flag that produced it. `expect-ref` below is a CROSS-CHECK
#   of the author's understanding against that measurement, not an input to it —
#   so this file can never make the harness believe something false about gcc.
#
# ★ AND DSS'S EXPECTED VERDICT IS NOT DECLARED AT ALL. It IS the measured
#   reference consensus — that is precisely the operator's rule, encoded. There is
#   deliberately no `expect-dss` key for a future edit to quietly relax.
#
# ── KEYS (an unknown key FAILS LOUD; a missing required key FAILS LOUD) ────
#
#   @probe <id>        unique; the census row name. Required.
#   @direction A|B     which direction this probe hunts. Required.
#   @mode strict|extended
#                      strict   → the ISO arm: gnu oracles add `-pedantic-errors`.
#                      extended → the permissive arm: gnu oracles use `-std=gnu*`
#                                 and NO -pedantic. ⚠ MSVC has no ISO-strict mode
#                                 at all, so for the msvc family the two modes are
#                                 the SAME command line — said out loud here rather
#                                 than papered over.
#   @min-stdc <n>      the probe is judged ONLY by oracles whose MEASURED
#                      `__STDC_VERSION__` is >= n. 0 = any. This is the mechanism
#                      that stops a C2x-DRAFT oracle from certifying C23: gcc 13.3
#                      measures 202000L, so `@min-stdc 202311` excludes it BY
#                      MEASUREMENT, with no per-compiler special case anywhere.
#   @families gnu|msvc|gnu,msvc
#                      which reference families are RELEVANT. MSVC having no
#                      `__attribute__` is not evidence that `__attribute__` is
#                      invented, so an `@direction A` probe may narrow. An
#                      `@direction B` probe may NOT narrow — "no reference compiler
#                      accepts it" is a union over ALL of them, and the harness
#                      rejects a narrowed B probe.
#   @expect-ref accept|reject|varies
#                      accept → at least one judging oracle must accept, else RED.
#                      reject → NO judging oracle may accept, else RED.
#                      varies → host/toolchain-dependent; NO assertion, and it must
#                               carry `@varies-note`. Every `varies` probe is PRINTED
#                               in the summary so this escape hatch cannot grow
#                               quietly.
#   @varies-note <text>   required iff `@expect-ref varies`; must be non-empty.
#   @acknowledged-gap <text>
#                      OPTIONAL. Presence means: a divergence on this probe is a
#                      KNOWN, RECORDED gap — census it, do not red. Absence means a
#                      divergence is a NEW defect and reds. ⚠ The ratchet only turns
#                      one way: when a gap is CLOSED the harness reds and names the
#                      exact line to delete, because an acknowledgement list that
#                      keeps stale rows is a guard that has been weakened until it
#                      asserts nothing.
#   @why <text>        required, non-empty; the citation or reason.
#   @code ... @end     the probe body. DECLARATIONS ONLY — no `#include`, no
#                      `main`, and every oracle compiles to a RELOCATABLE object
#                      (`-c` / `/c` / a bare DSS object format). System headers are
#                      the single largest confound available and are excluded by
#                      construction rather than by care.
#
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  HARNESS SANITY PAIR — the two probes that prove the harness can tell accept
#  from reject AT ALL, on EVERY oracle, on EVERY run. An oracle that fails either
#  one is reported BROKEN (red), never skipped: a driver whose quoting silently
#  collapsed would otherwise report "everything agrees" while compiling nothing.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

@probe sanity_accept
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why HARNESS SANITY: the most boring valid C declaration there is. Any oracle that rejects this is broken, not strict.
@code
int dss_sanity_accept_object;
@end

@probe sanity_reject
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@why HARNESS SANITY: illegal characters no C compiler accepts. Proves a nonzero verdict is reachable through the driver. It carries NO @acknowledged-gap: it is REJECTED BY DESIGN on every oracle AND on DSS, so it is an agreement row rather than a gap, and an acknowledgement here would have to be excused BY NAME in the stale-acknowledgement ratchet. A guard with a named exception is one edit away from having two.
@code
int @@@ dss_sanity_reject $$$ ;
@end

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  DIRECTION B — DSS ACCEPTS, NO REFERENCE COMPILER DOES.
#  The novel half, and the reason this corpus exists.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

@probe b_extern_library_realization_string
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap THE KNOWN INSTANCE, caught BY EYE by the operator and seeded here so the mechanism demonstrably catches it. DSS accepts a string literal as a declaration suffix meaning "this symbol is realized in that library" — self-described in the shipped examples as "DSS's own vocabulary for STATING a realization". It also contradicts the invariant recorded in src/hir/lowering/cst_to_hir.cpp (~line 2176): "a user declaration has authority over the SIGNATURE — never over the platform's REALIZATION." Removal is NOT this lane's work; the detector is.
@why ISO/IEC 9899:2024 §6.7.1 gives the COMPLETE production as `init-declarator: declarator | declarator = initializer` — nothing may follow a declarator but `=`, `,` or `;`. ⚠ Cite the grammar, NOT the diagnostic: gcc's message lists `asm` and `__attribute__` too, and both of those are gcc EXTENSIONS rather than part of the standard production, so the error text is a WIDER list than ISO C allows. The nearest reference-compiler spelling of this construct's intent is a GNU asm label (see a_asm_label_gnu), which every gnu oracle accepts — and which is an extension too, in scope here because the rule is what reference compilers support, not ISO only.
@code
extern int dss_realization_probe "msvcrt.dll";
@end

@probe b_extern_library_realization_string_function
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap Same construct as b_extern_library_realization_string in FUNCTION position. Kept as its own row because a fix that only closed the object-declaration path would leave this one open and the object-only probe would go green — a one-probe pin on a two-position grammar rule is a pin that can be half-satisfied.
@why Same rejection shape on every reference compiler; a string literal cannot follow a function declarator (an asm label needs the `__asm__`/`asm` keyword).
@code
extern int dss_realization_fn_probe(int) "msvcrt.dll";
@end

@probe b_extern_library_realization_string_decl_list
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap STRICTLY WIDER than b_extern_library_realization_string: the optional string sits AFTER the whole initDeclaratorList (/shapes/externDecl in c-subset.lang.json), so per that rule's own $comment it applies to EVERY declarator in the declaration. No corpus file uses this form — it is pure grammar over-admission on top of an already-invented construct.
@why A declaration-level realization suffix has no counterpart in C, GNU C or MSVC C.
@code
extern int dss_rl_a, *dss_rl_b, dss_rl_c[4] "msvcrt.dll";
@end

@probe b_extern_library_realization_wide_string
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap An accidental sub-case: the optional child is the full `stringLiteralExpr` shape, whose opener alt admits WideStringStart / Utf16 / Utf32 / Utf8 and a concatenation run. A library NAME is a byte string by nature, so admitting L"..." here is over-admission inside over-admission.
@why The position itself does not exist in any reference compiler, so no encoding prefix can make it legal.
@code
extern int dss_rl_wide "msv" L"crt.dll";
@end

@probe b_va_start_keywords_without_stdarg
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap DSS reserves va_start / va_arg / va_end as GRAMMAR KEYWORDS (/keywords VaStartKeyword, VaArgKeyword, VaEndKeyword; /shapes vaStartExpr, vaArgExpr, vaEndExpr) and injects __builtin_va_list unconditionally, so this compiles with NO <stdarg.h> at all. gcc/clang spell the primitives __builtin_va_start/__builtin_va_arg/__builtin_va_end; the unprefixed names are <stdarg.h> MACROS in every reference implementation. Note vaArgExpr also puts a TYPE-NAME in an expression argument position, which no reference grammar has outside a macro expansion.
@why Header-less: `va_start` is undeclared and `va_arg(ap, int)` is `expected expression before 'int'` on gcc/clang; MSVC likewise.
@code
int dss_va_probe(int n, ...) { __builtin_va_list ap; int v; va_start(ap, n); v = va_arg(ap, int); va_end(ap); return v; }
@end

@probe b_asm_label_after_attribute_and_duplicated
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@why /shapes/initDeclarator admits `{repeat: {alt: [attrSpec, stdAttr, asmLabel]}}`, i.e. EVERY interleaving and any number of asm labels. gcc and clang both fix the order as `declarator asm-label attributes` and reject a second label; MSVC has no asm label at all.
@code
extern int dss_asm_order_probe(void) __asm__("dss_first") __attribute__((weak)) __asm__("dss_second");
@end

@probe b_comma_operator_in_array_size
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@why /shapes/arrayDeclSuffix takes the full `expression` (comma at operator precedence 10) where ISO C 6.7.6.2 requires a constant-expression, which excludes the comma operator. DSS has a comma-free `assignmentExpr` shape and deliberately does not use it here.
@code
int dss_comma_array_probe[1, 2];
@end

@probe b_comma_operator_in_block_array_size
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap The BLOCK-SCOPE twin of b_comma_operator_in_array_size, and the sharper of the two. At file scope DSS rejects for an incidental reason ("a file-scope array requires a constant length"), which hides whether the COMMA was ever refused; inside a function there is no constant-length requirement to hide behind, so this row measures the grammar over-admission itself. Every reference compiler stops at the `,` with a pure SYNTAX error, at every -std level, because C 6.7.6.2 admits an assignment-expression there and not an expression.
@why gcc/clang: `expected ']' before ',' token`; MSVC: `syntax error: missing ']' before ','`.
@code
int dss_comma_block_array_probe(int n) { int a[1, 2]; a[0] = n; return a[0]; }
@end

@probe b_comma_operator_in_case_label
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED: DSS ACCEPTS `case 1, 2:` while all five oracles refuse it with a pure SYNTAX error (gcc `expected ':' or '...' before ','`, clang `expected ':' after 'case'`, cl `C2051 case expression not constant`). /shapes/caseLabel takes the full `expression`, so the comma operator is admitted where C 6.8.4.2 requires a constant-expression -- and the shape it most resembles is the multi-value case label that no C compiler has, so a reader could reasonably believe `case 1, 2:` matches both values.
@why /shapes/caseLabel takes the full `expression`; C 6.8.4.2 requires a constant-expression. `case 1, 2:` reads to a reference compiler as an attempt at the (non-existent) multi-value case label.
@code
int dss_comma_case_probe(int x) { switch (x) { case 1, 2: return 7; } return 0; }
@end

@probe b_comma_operator_in_designated_index
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@why /shapes/designatedIndex is `[ expression ]` where C 6.7.9 requires a constant-expression.
@code
int dss_comma_designator_probe[3] = {[0, 1] = 5};
@end

@probe b_empty_enumerator_list
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref varies
@varies-note ✔MEASURED and it is the reason this row is not a direction-B claim: cl 19.51 /std:clatest ACCEPTS `enum E { };` while gcc 13.2/13.3 say `empty enum is invalid` and clang 18/19 say `use of empty enum`. So a reference compiler DOES accept it — and on a host with no MSVC the same probe would look invented. A verdict that flips with which machine ran it must not be pinned in either direction.
@why /shapes/enumBody makes the enumerator list `{optional}`, which C 6.7.2.2 does not. Kept as a censused row precisely because the reference family SPLITS on it.
@code
enum DssEmptyEnum { };
@end

@probe b_elided_enumerator
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED: DSS ACCEPTS `enum F { A, , B };`. The `{optional: enumerator}` after Comma in /shapes/enumBody exists for the C99 TRAILING comma and also admits an INTERIOR hole. Note the contrast with b_empty_enumerator_list, where MSVC agrees with DSS -- here nothing does.
@why /shapes/enumBody spells the tail as `{repeat: {sequence: [Comma, {optional: enumerator}]}}`. The `{optional}` exists for the C99 TRAILING comma but also admits INTERIOR elision, which nothing accepts.
@code
enum DssElidedEnum { DSS_EE_A, , DSS_EE_B };
@end

@probe b_elided_initializer_element
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED: DSS ACCEPTS `{ 1, , 3 }`. Same `{optional}`-after-Comma shape as the enumerator list, in /shapes/braceInitList. This is the most dangerous of the elision family: the hole is SILENT, so the reader's third element lands at index 2 in DSS and the file does not compile anywhere else.
@why /shapes/braceInitList has the same `{optional}`-after-Comma shape as enumBody, so an interior hole in an initializer list is admitted.
@code
static int dss_elided_init_probe[3] = { 1, , 3 };
@end

@probe b_elided_struct_member_declarator
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED: DSS ACCEPTS `struct T { int a, , b; };`. /shapes/structMemberDeclarator is fully nullable. The FULLY empty member `struct T { int; };` IS caught (S_DeclarationDeclaresNothing) -- it is the populated-list-with-a-hole case that slips past, which is exactly the shape a guard written against the empty case never sees.
@why /shapes/structMemberDeclarator is fully nullable (optional declarator + optional bit-field suffix), so a mid-list hole is admitted. The FULLY empty case `struct T { int; };` IS caught (S_DeclarationDeclaresNothing) — a populated list with a hole in it is the case that slips past.
@code
struct DssElidedMember { int a, , b; };
@end

@probe b_ellipsis_not_last_parameter
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED: DSS ACCEPTS `int f(int a, ..., int b);`. /shapes/paramList repeats `{Comma, paramOrEllipsis}` with no rule that the ellipsis terminates the list (C 6.7.6.3), and no semantic check supplies one.
@why /shapes/paramList repeats `{Comma, paramOrEllipsis}` with no "the ellipsis terminates the list" rule. C 6.7.6.3 puts `...` last, full stop.
@code
int dss_ellipsis_mid_probe(int a, ..., int b);
@end

@probe b_duplicate_array_param_qualifiers
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED: DSS ACCEPTS `int a[static static const restrict const 3]`. /shapes/arrayDeclSuffix repeats `{alt: [static, const, volatile, restrict]}` unbounded with no duplicate check. gcc/clang report `duplicate 'static'`/`duplicate 'const'`; the NON-parameter use of the same form IS caught (S_ArrayParamQualifierNonParameter), so the guard exists one axis over.
@why /shapes/arrayDeclSuffix repeats `{alt: [static, const, volatile, restrict]}` with no duplicate check. gcc/clang report `duplicate 'static'` / `duplicate 'const'`; MSVC rejects the whole C99 array-parameter form.
@code
int dss_dup_arrayqual_probe(int a[static static const restrict const 3]);
@end

@probe b_assignment_inside_attribute_argument
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@why /shapes/attrArgItem admits `attrArgAtom {optional {AssignOp, attrArgAtom}}` for EVERY attribute. Its own $comment scopes the intent to clang's `__availability__(..., message=_msg)`; the slot is generic. gcc accepts no `=` in any attribute argument, and clang accepts it only inside `availability`.
@code
int dss_attr_assign_probe __attribute__((aligned=8));
@end

@probe b_msvc_calling_convention_macro_without_windows_h
@direction B
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref reject
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED: `int WINAPI f(void);` is ACCEPTED by DSS for the pe64 target and REJECTED by all seven oracles — cl 19.51 included, because MSVC itself needs <windows.h> for WINAPI. This is the row that justifies the two-fixed-target design: DSS rejects it for elf64 and accepts it for pe64, so "accepted under ANY target" is what identifies it as an invented construct rather than a per-target detail. A source file written against these predefines compiles only with DSS, and only for pe.
@why /preprocess/predefinedMacros predefines WINAPI, __stdcall, __cdecl, __fastcall and __forceinline to the EMPTY STRING, gated availableObjectFormats ["pe"]. So a pe translation unit sees them with NO <windows.h>. MSVC needs <windows.h>/<minwindef.h> for WINAPI; gcc/clang have no such macro at any -std level. (This probe is why DSS's verdict is measured for TWO fixed object formats: it is admitted for pe and rejected for elf, and "accepted under any target" is what makes a construct invented.)
@code
int WINAPI dss_winapi_probe(void);
@end

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  DIRECTION A — a reference compiler ACCEPTS, DSS may REJECT.
#  ISO surface first, then the extensions reference compilers ship. The rule is
#  NOT ISO-only, so `__attribute__` / asm labels / __int128 / __try / typeof are
#  IN SCOPE here exactly as much as `_Generic` is.
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── C11 surface (every oracle here measures >= 201112L) ────────────────────

@probe a_generic_c11
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 201112
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C11 6.5.1.1 _Generic primary expression.
@code
int dss_generic_probe(void) { double d = 0.0; return _Generic(d, double: 1, default: 0); }
@end

@probe a_static_assert_c11
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 201112
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C11 6.7.10 _Static_assert with a message.
@code
_Static_assert(sizeof(int) >= 2, "int is at least 16 bits");
@end

@probe a_alignas_alignof_c11
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 201112
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C11 6.7.5 _Alignas and 6.5.3.4 _Alignof.
@code
_Alignas(16) int dss_alignas_probe;
int dss_alignof_probe = (int)_Alignof(double);
@end

@probe a_anonymous_struct_member_c11
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 201112
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C11 6.7.2.1p13 anonymous structure/union members.
@code
struct DssAnon { int tag; union { int i; float f; }; };
int dss_anon_probe(struct DssAnon *p) { return p->i; }
@end

@probe a_atomic_qualifier_c11
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 201112
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why C11 6.7.3 _Atomic type qualifier. Narrowed to gnu: MSVC gates C11 atomics behind /experimental:c11atomics, which is a compiler-configuration fact rather than evidence about the construct.
@code
_Atomic int dss_atomic_probe;
@end

@probe a_noreturn_c11
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 201112
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C11 7.23 _Noreturn function specifier.
@code
_Noreturn void dss_noreturn_probe(void);
@end

@probe a_thread_local_definition_c11
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 201112
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap, found 2026-08-12 while censusing the `__thread` spelling and NOT previously in this corpus. The refusal is NOT in the front end and that is the whole point of the row: DSS PARSES and lowers `static _Thread_local int x;` and then the LINKER refuses it — `K_NoMatchingObjectFormat`: "module carries an AssembledData item with section=tbss but format 'elf64-x86_64-linux' (kind=elf) does not advertise that section in its 'supportedDataSections' set". Identical refusal on the pe64 target. ✔MEASURED: `tbss` is advertised by EXACTLY FOUR shipped formats, all of them `*-exec` (elf64-x86_64-linux-exec, elf64-aarch64-linux-exec, pe64-x86_64-windows-exec, macho64-arm64-darwin-exec), so a thread-local DEFINITION reaches no relocatable-object, PIE, shared-library or staticlib arm — and note macho64-x86_64-darwin-exec is absent from even that list. The four `D-CSUBSET-THREAD-LOCAL` legs were closed for EXECUTABLES; this corpus compiles to relocatable objects (`-c` / `/c`), which is the arm nothing covered. It is LOUD, never a silent miscompile. Tracked at D-LK-RELOCATABLE-TBSS-UNSUPPORTED. ⚠ Do NOT "close" this row by giving the probe an `extern` body: an extern declaration emits no data item at all, so it would sail past the linker and the row would silently stop measuring anything (that is exactly why a_thread_local_gnu_spelling uses `extern` — it must isolate the SPELLING from this gap, and this row must isolate this gap from the spelling).
@why C11 6.7.1 `_Thread_local` storage-class specifier on a file-scope DEFINITION. Accepted by gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3, clang 19.1.1 (`-std=c17 -pedantic-errors`) and cl 19.51 (`/std:c17` and `/std:clatest`).
@code
static _Thread_local int dss_thread_local_definition_probe;
@end

# ── C99 surface ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@probe a_compound_literal_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.5.2.5 compound literals.
@code
struct DssPoint { int x, y; };
int dss_compound_probe(void) { struct DssPoint p = (struct DssPoint){1, 2}; return p.x + p.y; }
@end

@probe a_designated_initializers_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.7.9 designated initializers, both member and array-index forms.
@code
struct DssD { int a, b; };
struct DssD dss_desig_probe = {.b = 2, .a = 1};
int dss_desig_arr[4] = {[2] = 7, [0] = 1};
@end

@probe a_flexible_array_member_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.7.2.1p18 flexible array member.
@code
struct DssFam { int n; char data[]; };
int dss_fam_probe(struct DssFam *p) { return p->n; }
@end

@probe a_restrict_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.7.3 restrict qualifier.
@code
void dss_restrict_probe(int *restrict a, int *restrict b) { *a = *b; }
@end

@probe a_vla_parameter_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.7.6.2 variable-length array. Narrowed to gnu: MSVC has never implemented VLAs, and C11 6.10.8.3 makes them optional (__STDC_NO_VLA__), so an MSVC rejection is not evidence about the construct.
@code
int dss_vla_probe(int n) { int a[n]; a[0] = n; return a[0]; }
@end

@probe a_complex_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.2.5 _Complex. Narrowed to gnu: C11 makes complex optional (__STDC_NO_COMPLEX__) and MSVC implements the _Dcomplex struct family instead of the _Complex keyword.
@code
_Complex double dss_complex_probe;
@end

@probe a_mixed_declarations_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.8.2 declarations may follow statements inside a block, and 6.8.5.3 a for-loop may declare its counter.
@code
int dss_mixed_probe(int n) { int t = n; t = t + 1; int u = t; for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i) u += i; return u; }
@end

@probe a_func_predefined_ident_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.4.2.2 the predefined identifier __func__.
@code
char const *dss_func_probe(void) { return __func__; }
@end

@probe a_variadic_macro_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.10.3 variadic function-like macros with __VA_ARGS__.
@code
#define DSS_SUM(...) dss_variadic_sink(__VA_ARGS__)
int dss_variadic_sink(int, int);
int dss_variadic_probe(void) { return DSS_SUM(1, 2); }
@end

# ── C23 surface. `@min-stdc 202311` is what keeps a C2x-DRAFT oracle out. ──

@probe a_nullptr_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.4.4.6 nullptr / 7.21 nullptr_t.
@code
void *dss_nullptr_probe = nullptr;
@end

@probe a_bool_true_false_keywords_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.4.1 bool/true/false are keywords; no <stdbool.h> required.
@code
bool dss_bool_probe = true;
bool dss_bool_probe2 = false;
@end

@probe a_static_assert_no_message_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.7.11 static_assert spelling with the message omitted.
@code
static_assert(sizeof(int) >= 2);
@end

@probe a_typeof_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.7.2.5 typeof and typeof_unqual.
@code
int dss_typeof_src;
typeof(dss_typeof_src) dss_typeof_probe;
typeof_unqual(int const) dss_typeof_unqual_probe;
@end

@probe a_bitint_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.2.5p5 _BitInt(N). Narrowed to gnu: MSVC has no _BitInt as of cl 19.5x, and that is a compiler-coverage fact, not evidence about the construct.
@code
_BitInt(37) dss_bitint_probe;
unsigned _BitInt(3) dss_bitint_probe2;
@end

@probe a_binary_literal_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.4.4.1 binary integer constants (0b/0B).
@code
int dss_binary_literal_probe = 0b1011;
@end

@probe a_enum_fixed_underlying_type_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.7.2.2 enum with a fixed underlying type. ⚠ NO enum-typed GLOBAL here on purpose: the first draft of this probe declared one and DSS rejected it at `lowerMirGlobalsToDataItems` (K_NoMatchingObjectFormat, D-LK4-RODATA-PRODUCER-AGGREGATE-GLOBAL) — a BACK-END limitation on non-primitive global types that has nothing to do with the C23 syntax under test. A probe that reports a real rejection for the wrong reason is a false census row, so the enumerator is consumed inside a function instead.
@code
enum DssFixed : unsigned char { DSS_FIXED_A = 1, DSS_FIXED_B = 2 };
int dss_enum_fixed_probe(void) { return (int)DSS_FIXED_B; }
@end

@probe a_standard_attribute_leading_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.7.12.1 [[deprecated]] in the LEADING declaration position. Split from the parameter form below: the first draft carried both in one probe, so a single rejection could not say WHICH position DSS lacks — and "one construct per probe" is what makes a census row actionable.
@code
[[deprecated]] int dss_attr_deprecated_probe(void);
@end

@probe a_standard_attribute_parameter_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: `[[maybe_unused]] int p` in a PARAMETER declaration is rejected by DSS while clang 18/19 (C23) and cl /std:clatest accept it. Its LEADING-position twin (a_standard_attribute_leading_c23) is accepted, so this is a position-specific hole, not absent C23 attribute support. RECORDED, not fixed -- this lane builds the detector.
@why C23 6.7.12.1 [[maybe_unused]] on a PARAMETER declaration.
@code
int dss_attr_param_probe([[maybe_unused]] int unused_param);
@end

@probe a_empty_initializer_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.7.10 the empty initializer `= {}`.
@code
struct DssEmptyInit { int a; int b; };
struct DssEmptyInit dss_empty_init_probe = {};
@end

@probe a_va_opt_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.10.5.1 __VA_OPT__ (IMPLEMENTED, FC18a / D-PP-VA-OPT). The `@acknowledged-gap` line that stood here is DELETED: DSS now expands the construct and agrees with the oracles. Semantics + fail-loud arms are pinned by the `PreprocessorVaOpt.*` unit suite; the runtime witness is examples/c-subset/preprocessor_va_opt. clang-18 rejects THIS probe for a different, measured reason (-Wgnu-zero-variadic-macro-arguments promoted by -pedantic-errors on `DSS_CALL(1)`), which is why the direction rule is `any judging oracle accepts` rather than `all`.
@code
int dss_vaopt_sink(int);
#define DSS_CALL(a, ...) dss_vaopt_sink(a __VA_OPT__(+) __VA_ARGS__)
int dss_vaopt_probe(void) { return DSS_CALL(1) + DSS_CALL(1, 2); }
@end

@probe a_va_opt_paste_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.10.5.1p7 + 6.10.5.3p3 -- a `__VA_OPT__` that is an operand of an OUTSIDE `##`. With no variable arguments the va-opt is a PLACEMARKER, so the paste yields the other operand instead of violating the no-dangling-`##` constraint. Split from a_va_opt_c23 because it exercises the placemarker interaction rather than plain presence/absence, and one construct per probe is what makes a census row actionable.
@code
int dss_vaopt_base;
int dss_vaopt_base_x;
#define DSS_PICK(...) dss_vaopt_base ## __VA_OPT__(_x)
int dss_vaopt_paste_probe(void) { return DSS_PICK(1) + DSS_PICK(0); }
@end

@probe a_va_opt_stringize_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.10.5.1p4 + 6.10.5.2 -- `#__VA_OPT__( ... )`. A va-opt-replacement is "treated as if it were a parameter", so it is a valid `#` operand; the spelling keeps the content's ORIGINAL adjacency (white space between tokens becomes one space, and none is inserted where there was none).
@code
extern int dss_vaopt_str_sink(const char*);
#define DSS_STR(X, ...) #__VA_OPT__(X+X)
int dss_vaopt_str_probe(void) { return dss_vaopt_str_sink(DSS_STR(p, 1)); }
@end

@probe a_elifdef_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.10.1 #elifdef / #elifndef.
@code
#define DSS_ELIFDEF_ON 1
#ifdef DSS_NOT_DEFINED_AT_ALL
int dss_elifdef_probe = 1;
#elifdef DSS_ELIFDEF_ON
int dss_elifdef_probe = 2;
#else
int dss_elifdef_probe = 3;
#endif
@end

@probe a_unnamed_parameter_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.7.6.3 a parameter may be unnamed in a function DEFINITION.
@code
int dss_unnamed_param_probe(int, double) { return 0; }
@end

@probe a_label_at_block_end_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.8.1 a label may appear at the end of a compound statement, and before a declaration. ⚠ Declared `void`, deliberately: the first draft returned `int` and fell off the end after the trailing label, so DSS answered H0003 "non-void function may fall through without returning a value" — a true diagnostic about a DIFFERENT defect in the probe, which would have censused as a C23-label gap.
@code
void dss_label_probe(int n) { if (n) goto done; int t = n + 1; (void)t; done: ; }
@end

@probe a_constexpr_object_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.7.1 the constexpr storage-class specifier on an object definition.
@code
constexpr int dss_constexpr_probe = 42;
@end

@probe a_u8_char_literal_c23
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23 6.4.4.4 u8 character constants.
@code
unsigned char dss_u8_char_probe = u8'A';
@end

# ── Extensions the reference compilers ship. IN SCOPE: the rule is not ISO-only.

@probe a_attribute_packed_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU __attribute__((packed)) on a struct. Narrowed to gnu: MSVC spells this #pragma pack (probe a_pragma_pack).
@code
struct DssPacked { char c; int i; } __attribute__((packed));
int dss_packed_probe(void) { return (int)sizeof(struct DssPacked); }
@end

@probe a_attribute_aligned_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU __attribute__((aligned(N))) on an object.
@code
int dss_aligned_probe __attribute__((aligned(16)));
@end

@probe a_attribute_noreturn_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU __attribute__((noreturn)) on a function declaration.
@code
void dss_attr_noreturn_probe(void) __attribute__((noreturn));
@end

@probe a_asm_label_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU asm label — the reference-compiler spelling of "this declaration is realized under that external name". It is the construct probe b_extern_library_realization_string should have been.
@code
extern int dss_asm_label_probe __asm__("dss_external_name");
@end

@probe a_int128_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU __int128 on a 64-bit target.
@code
__int128 dss_int128_probe;
unsigned __int128 dss_uint128_probe;
@end

@probe a_typeof_gnu_spelling
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU __typeof__ — available at every -std level, unlike the C23 `typeof` keyword. CLOSED 2026-08-11 (D-CSUBSET-TYPEOF-GNU-SPELLING): `__typeof__` and `__typeof` are now keyword-table aliases onto the EXISTING TypeofKeyword, so they reach the same qualifier-PRESERVING semantic arm the C23 spelling does — no new semantics, no C++ change. The second spelling is exercised below on purpose: it also proves the lexer takes the LONGEST match rather than reading `__typeof__` as `__typeof` plus a stray identifier.
@code
int dss_typeof_gnu_src;
__typeof__(dss_typeof_gnu_src) dss_typeof_gnu_probe;
__typeof(dss_typeof_gnu_src) dss_typeof_gnu_probe2;
@end

@probe a_statement_expression_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: GNU statement expressions `({ ... })` are rejected by DSS and accepted by all four gnu oracles. Pervasive in glibc/kernel-style headers and in macro-heavy corpora.
@why GNU statement expressions `({ ... })`.
@code
int dss_stmt_expr_probe(int n) { return ({ int t = n; t + 1; }); }
@end

@probe a_builtin_expect_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: `__builtin_expect` is an unknown identifier to DSS (S0001) and accepted by all four gnu oracles. It appears throughout real C corpora, sqlite included.
@why GNU __builtin_expect — pervasive in real C corpora (sqlite, glibc headers).
@code
int dss_builtin_expect_probe(int n) { return __builtin_expect(n != 0, 1) ? 1 : 0; }
@end

@probe a_zero_length_array_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU zero-length arrays as struct members — the pre-C99 spelling of a flexible array member, still in wide use.
@code
struct DssZeroLen { int n; char data[0]; };
int dss_zero_len_probe(struct DssZeroLen *p) { return p->n; }
@end

@probe a_case_range_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: GNU case ranges `case 1 ... 5:` are rejected by DSS (`expected 'Colon' -- got '...'`) and accepted by all four gnu oracles.
@why GNU case ranges `case 1 ... 5:`.
@code
int dss_case_range_probe(int n) { switch (n) { case 1 ... 5: return 1; default: return 0; } }
@end

@probe a_label_as_value_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU labels as values (`&&label` and a `void *` computed goto) — the classic threaded-interpreter dispatch idiom.
@code
int dss_label_value_probe(int n) { void *p = &&target; if (n) goto *p; return 0; target: return 1; }
@end

@probe a_nested_function_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref varies
@varies-note A gcc-only extension: gcc accepts a nested function definition, clang has never implemented it and rejects. Both are the `gnu` family, so the family-level verdict genuinely differs BY COMPILER and any single declared expectation would be false on some host.
@why GNU nested functions.
@code
int dss_nested_fn_probe(int n) { int inner(int x) { return x + 1; } return inner(n); }
@end

@probe a_pragma_pack
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why #pragma pack(N) / pop — the one packed-struct spelling BOTH families implement.
@code
#pragma pack(push, 1)
struct DssPragmaPacked { char c; int i; };
#pragma pack(pop)
int dss_pragma_pack_probe(void) { return (int)sizeof(struct DssPragmaPacked); }
@end

@probe a_pragma_operator_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.10.9 the _Pragma operator.
@code
_Pragma("pack(push, 1)")
struct DssPragmaOp { char c; int i; };
_Pragma("pack(pop)")
int dss_pragma_op_probe(void) { return (int)sizeof(struct DssPragmaOp); }
@end

@probe a_declspec_msvc
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why MSVC __declspec(dllimport). Narrowed to msvc as the family that DEFINES it; a mingw gcc accepts it too and that shows up in the measured per-oracle rows.
@code
__declspec(dllimport) int dss_declspec_probe;
@end

@probe a_seh_try_except_msvc
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families msvc
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap NOT a front-end gap, and the distinction is the whole value of this row. DSS PARSES `__try`/`__except` (it reaches lowering) and then refuses at `synthesizeSehFunclets` because neither fixed probe target declares a `sehPersonality` block: the pe64 RELOCATABLE-OBJECT arm emits no unwind info, so accepting would be a silent miscompile in which `__except` can never run (D-LK-PE-OBJ-ARM-CARRIES-NO-UNWIND-INFO, D-WIN64-SEH-FUNCLETS). It is a LOUD, CORRECT format-capability refusal. It is censused rather than dropped because the user-visible fact is real — you cannot compile `__try` into a pe64 .obj with DSS while cl.exe can — and because this harness compiles to relocatable objects on purpose (the exact `-c` / `/c` stage the reference compilers are held to). ⚠ Do NOT "close" this by adding a personality block to an object format; that is the refusal's own documented trap.
@why MSVC structured exception handling __try / __except. DSS implements SEH .pdata/.xdata for the pe64 exec and dll formats, so this is squarely in scope.
@code
int dss_seh_probe(int n) { int r = 0; __try { r = 1 / n; } __except (1) { r = -1; } return r; }
@end

@probe a_forceinline_msvc
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why MSVC __forceinline function specifier.
@code
__forceinline int dss_forceinline_probe(int n) { return n + 1; }
@end

@probe a_counter_macro
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: `__COUNTER__` is undefined in DSS (S0001) and predefined by ALL FIVE oracles -- the only probe in this corpus that every located reference compiler accepts and DSS refuses.
@why __COUNTER__ — a gcc/clang/MSVC-common predefined macro used for unique-name generation.
@code
int dss_counter_probe_a = __COUNTER__;
int dss_counter_probe_b = __COUNTER__;
@end

@probe a_inline_asm_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: DSS's /shapes/asmStmt admits an asm TEMPLATE only, so the operand-constraint form `__asm__ volatile("" : "=r"(r) : "r"(n))` stops at the first `:`. All four gnu oracles accept it. The template-only subset is what real corpora almost never use.
@why GNU extended inline asm with operand constraints — the form real corpora use.
@code
int dss_inline_asm_probe(int n) { int r; __asm__ volatile("" : "=r"(r) : "r"(n)); return r; }
@end

@probe a_stray_toplevel_semicolon
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why A stray file-scope `;` is accepted by gcc/clang/MSVC outside -pedantic-errors. `@mode extended` is load-bearing here rather than decorative: under the strict arm the same probe is a pedantic ERROR, so judging it strictly would manufacture a false direction-A row.
@code
int dss_stray_semi_probe;
;
;;
@end

@probe a_empty_struct_body_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why An empty struct body is a GNU extension (and an MSVC one); it is a pedantic error under ISO C, hence the extended arm.
@code
struct DssEmptyBody { };
int dss_empty_body_probe(void) { return (int)sizeof(struct DssEmptyBody); }
@end

@probe a_enum_underlying_typeof_c23
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 202311
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C23's fixed enum underlying type composed with C23 `typeof` — the two features meeting, which is where a grammar that bolted them on separately shows the seam.
@code
enum DssUnderTypeof : typeof(1) { DSS_UT_A = 1 };
@end

@probe a_designated_initializer_nested_c99
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C99 6.7.9 nested designators (`.outer.inner =` and `[i].member =`), the form real corpora use for tables of structs.
@code
struct DssInner { int a, b; };
struct DssOuter { struct DssInner in; int tail; };
struct DssOuter dss_nested_desig_probe = {.in.b = 2, .tail = 3};
struct DssInner dss_nested_arr_probe[2] = {[1].a = 5};
@end

@probe a_string_literal_concatenation
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C 6.4.5 adjacent string literal concatenation, including across a macro boundary — pervasive in real corpora.
@code
#define DSS_SUFFIX "world"
char const dss_concat_probe[] = "hello " DSS_SUFFIX "!";
@end

@probe a_bitfield_declarations
@direction A
@mode strict
@min-stdc 199901
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@why C 6.7.2.1 bit-fields, including a zero-width unnamed one (the forced-alignment idiom) and an unnamed padding field.
@code
struct DssBits { unsigned a : 3; unsigned : 0; unsigned b : 5; signed c : 2; };
int dss_bits_probe(struct DssBits *p) { return (int)p->a + (int)p->b; }
@end

@probe a_local_label_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: GNU local labels (`__label__ L;` inside a block) are rejected by DSS and accepted by gcc 13.3.0 and clang 19.1.1. The companion of the computed-goto machinery DSS already has, and the form macro-heavy corpora use to keep a label private to a macro expansion.
@why GNU C `__label__` declares a label local to the enclosing block.
@code
int dss_local_label_probe(void) { __label__ done; goto done; done: return 0; }
@end

@probe a_builtin_types_compatible_p_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: rejected by DSS, accepted by gcc 13.3.0 and clang 19.1.1. It is the primitive every pre-C11 type-generic macro is written on, and it takes TYPE NAMES in an expression argument position — the same grammatical shape as `_Generic`, which DSS does support (a_generic_c11 passes).
@why GNU `__builtin_types_compatible_p(T1, T2)`.
@code
int dss_types_compatible_probe(void) { return __builtin_types_compatible_p(int, long); }
@end

@probe a_builtin_choose_expr_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: rejected by DSS, accepted by gcc 13.3.0 and clang 19.1.1. Paired with __builtin_types_compatible_p above, this is how type-generic macros SELECT a branch without evaluating the other one.
@why GNU `__builtin_choose_expr(const, a, b)`.
@code
int dss_choose_expr_probe(int n) { return __builtin_choose_expr(1, n, 0); }
@end

@probe a_builtin_offsetof_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: rejected by DSS, accepted by gcc 13.3.0 and clang 19.1.1. This is what <stddef.h>'s `offsetof` EXPANDS TO on both of them, so a translation unit reaching it through the standard header hits this gap without ever writing `__builtin_offsetof`.
@why GNU `__builtin_offsetof(type, member)`.
@code
struct DssOffs { int a, b; };
unsigned long dss_builtin_offsetof_probe(void) { return __builtin_offsetof(struct DssOffs, b); }
@end

@probe a_real_imag_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: rejected by DSS, accepted by gcc 13.3.0 and clang 19.1.1. Note DSS DOES accept the `_Complex` type itself (a_complex_c99 passes) — so complex values can be declared and not decomposed, which is the shape of a half-implemented feature rather than an absent one.
@why GNU `__real__` / `__imag__` operators on a complex value.
@code
_Complex double dss_real_imag_src;
double dss_real_imag_probe(void) { return __real__ dss_real_imag_src + __imag__ dss_real_imag_src; }
@end

@probe a_alignof_gnu_spelling
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU `__alignof__`. CLOSED 2026-08-11 (D-CSUBSET-ALIGNOF-GNU-SPELLING): `__alignof__` and `__alignof` are now keyword-table aliases onto the EXISTING AlignofKeyword, so both reach the same `alignofType` rule `_Alignof` does. TYPE-NAME operand only — the VALUE operand every oracle also accepts is a separate, PRE-EXISTING gap that hits `_Alignof` identically and is censused by a_alignof_expression_operand_gnu below.
@code
int dss_alignof_gnu_probe(void) { return (int)__alignof__(double); }
int dss_alignof_gnu_probe2(void) { return (int)__alignof(double); }
@end

@probe a_alignof_expression_operand_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap, found 2026-08-11 while closing a_alignof_gnu_spelling and NOT previously censused — the sibling probe only ever exercised the TYPE-NAME operand, so the operand axis was invisible to the whole corpus. gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3 and clang 19.1.1 all accept an EXPRESSION operand (measured with matched positive+negative controls, at -std=gnu17 and at each oracle's newest -std), and they accept it for EVERY spelling — `__alignof__(d)`, `__alignof(d)`, `_Alignof(d)`, `alignof(d)` via <stdalign.h>, and even the paren-free `_Alignof d`. DSS accepts NONE of them. This is therefore NOT a GNU-spelling gap: it hits the already-shipped `_Alignof` exactly as hard, and the new aliases neither caused it nor widened it. `alignofType` is type-name-only by construction (a keyword row cannot reach the value form), so closing it means an `alignofValue` rule plus an `alignofExpr` wrapper — the sizeofExpr/sizeofType/sizeofValue triple, verbatim — and a `valueRule` arm in the alignof semantics block. Tracked at D-CSUBSET-ALIGNOF-VALUE-OPERAND. Today it fails LOUD at parse; there is no wrong-alignment path.
@why GNU/ISO alignof applied to an EXPRESSION rather than a type-name — the operand axis, not the spelling axis.
@code
double dss_alignof_expr_src;
int dss_alignof_expr_probe(void) { return (int)__alignof__(dss_alignof_expr_src); }
@end

@probe a_volatile_gnu_spelling
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU `__volatile__` / `__volatile` — the qualifier spelling that works at EVERY -std level. UNCENSUSED UNTIL 2026-08-12: the whole `__`-wrapped QUALIFIER class was missing from this corpus, which had probes for the GNU `__typeof__`/`__alignof__`/`__inline__` spellings but none for volatile/const/signed/restrict/complex — so five direction-A gaps were invisible rather than absent, exactly the way the alignof OPERAND axis was. CLOSED the day it was censused (D-CSUBSET-GNU-DUNDER-QUALIFIER-SPELLINGS): both spellings are keyword-table aliases onto the EXISTING VolatileKeyword, so they reach the interned Volatile qualifier bit the ISO word reaches — no new kind, no grammar, no C++. Both spellings are exercised below, which also proves the lexer takes the LONGEST match (`__volatile__` is not read as `__volatile` plus a stray `__`), and BOTH grammar slots are exercised: the declaration head (`headQualifier`) and after a `*` (`ptrQualifier`), because a row can be reachable from one and not the other. MEASURED accepted by gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3, clang 19.1.1 — and by `gcc -pedantic-errors`, since a dunder spelling is in C 7.1.3's implementation-reserved namespace and cannot collide with a conforming program's identifiers. That is why this needed no dialect flag. ⚠ NOT claimed here: real `cl.exe` REFUSES the dunder qualifier forms; two of three reference compilers is the bar this corpus applies.
@code
__volatile__ int dss_volatile_gnu_probe;
__volatile int dss_volatile_gnu_probe2;
int * __volatile__ dss_volatile_gnu_ptr;
int * __volatile dss_volatile_gnu_ptr2;
@end

@probe a_const_gnu_spelling
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU `__const__` / `__const` — the pre-C89-portable spelling of `const`, still emitted by generator-produced headers. CLOSED 2026-08-12 (D-CSUBSET-GNU-DUNDER-QUALIFIER-SPELLINGS): keyword-table aliases onto the EXISTING ConstKeyword, so they reach `constMarker` and an assignment through one is S_ConstViolation exactly as through `const` — the SEMANTIC effect, not merely a parse. Three grammar positions below: declaration head, EAST/trailing specifier (C 6.7.1 lets decl-specifiers appear in any order), and after a `*`. MEASURED accepted by gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3, clang 19.1.1 and by `gcc -pedantic-errors`. A parameter-position use is exercised too, since parameters take a different head rule than file-scope declarations.
@code
__const__ int dss_const_gnu_probe = 1;
__const int dss_const_gnu_probe2 = 2;
int __const__ dss_const_gnu_east = 3;
int * __const__ dss_const_gnu_ptr;
void dss_const_gnu_param(__const__ int a, __const int b);
@end

@probe a_signed_gnu_spelling
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU `__signed__` / `__signed` — the pre-C89-portable spelling of the `signed` type-specifier. CLOSED 2026-08-12 (D-CSUBSET-GNU-DUNDER-QUALIFIER-SPELLINGS): keyword-table aliases onto the EXISTING SignedKeyword, so they participate in the same type-specifier MULTISET rows the ISO word does — `__signed__ char` resolves I8, NOT the U8 an UnsignedKeyword mis-key would give nor the I32 a dropped specifier would give. Three multiset shapes below: with `char`, BARE (== `signed int`), and in the multi-token `long` combination, because a specifier alias must reach every row it belongs to and not merely the first. MEASURED accepted by gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3, clang 19.1.1 and by `gcc -pedantic-errors`.
@code
__signed__ char dss_signed_gnu_probe;
__signed char dss_signed_gnu_probe2;
__signed__ int dss_signed_gnu_int;
__signed__ dss_signed_gnu_bare;
__signed dss_signed_gnu_bare2;
__signed__ long dss_signed_gnu_long;
@end

@probe a_restrict_gnu_spelling
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU `__restrict__` / `__restrict` — the spelling that works below C99, which is why libstdc++/glibc headers use it in preference to `restrict`. CLOSED 2026-08-12 (D-CSUBSET-GNU-DUNDER-QUALIFIER-SPELLINGS): keyword-table aliases onto the EXISTING RestrictKeyword, reachable from `ptrQualifier` and from the array-suffix modifier list, exercised in every one of those positions below (post-star object, two in one signature, mixed spellings in one signature, and the `int a[__restrict__ 4]` array-parameter slot). ★ NOTE FOR WHOEVER READS THIS NEXT: `restrict` — ISO OR GNU — is NOT admitted in DECLARATION-HEAD position (`__restrict__ int x;`), because `headQualifier` is {const, volatile, _Atomic}. That is PRE-EXISTING and correct: C 6.7.3 restricts the qualifier to pointer types, so head position has nothing to qualify. It is also the property that PINS these rows to RestrictKeyword rather than to a plausible neighbour — Const/Volatile/Atomic/Signed/Complex are all accepted bare in front of a base type, so a mis-keyed row would have been accepted there. MEASURED accepted by gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3, clang 19.1.1 and by `gcc -pedantic-errors`.
@code
int * __restrict__ dss_restrict_gnu_probe;
int * __restrict dss_restrict_gnu_probe2;
void dss_restrict_gnu_sig(int * __restrict__ a, int * __restrict__ b);
void dss_restrict_gnu_mixed(int * __restrict__ a, int * __restrict b);
void dss_restrict_gnu_array(int a[__restrict__ 4], int b[__restrict 4]);
@end

@probe a_complex_gnu_spelling
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why GNU `__complex__` / `__complex` — the pre-C99-portable spelling of `_Complex`. CLOSED 2026-08-12 (D-CSUBSET-GNU-DUNDER-QUALIFIER-SPELLINGS): keyword-table aliases onto the EXISTING ComplexKeyword, so they participate in the same type-specifier multiset rows and resolve to a Complex type over the right ELEMENT (`__complex__ double` → F64 element, `__complex__ float` → F32). Both spellings, both element widths, and the EAST/trailing position below. The mis-key this guards against is the ADJACENT ImaginaryKeyword, which sits in NO multiset row: an `__complex__` landed there would resolve as an unknown type name instead. MEASURED accepted by gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3, clang 19.1.1 and by `gcc -pedantic-errors`, including `__complex__ long double`.
@code
__complex__ double dss_complex_gnu_probe;
__complex double dss_complex_gnu_probe2;
double __complex__ dss_complex_gnu_east;
__complex__ float dss_complex_gnu_float;
__complex float dss_complex_gnu_float2;
@end

@probe a_asm_volatile_gnu_spelling
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@why `__asm__ __volatile__ ("")` — the GNU qualifier spelling INSIDE the inline-asm statement, which is the form real headers write. This is a SECOND-ORDER consequence of the `__volatile__` keyword row and it gets its own probe because it is a different GRAMMAR SLOT: `asmStmt`'s `{optional VolatileKeyword}`, not a declaration qualifier position. CLOSED 2026-08-12 as a side effect of D-CSUBSET-GNU-DUNDER-QUALIFIER-SPELLINGS, which also closed the `__volatile__` half of D-CSUBSET-INLINE-ASM-SPELLING. ⚠ WHAT IS *NOT* CLOSED, stated here so the half-closure is never read as a whole one: bare `asm` is still deliberately absent (it is an ordinary identifier in standard C, so reserving it would take a name user code may have defined — that one genuinely needs a dialect opt-in), and the `:` OPERAND LIST is a separate open gap censused by a_inline_asm_gnu above (D-CSUBSET-INLINE-ASM-OPERANDS / D-LANG-GNU-EXTENDED-INLINE-ASM-UNSUPPORTED). sqlite's `src/hwtime.h:43` needs BOTH, so its `--scanstatus` build stays off; this row moved that file's failure from the spelling to the colon and nothing further. Only the EMPTY template is probed here — a non-empty one carries per-target instructions DSS cannot emit and is refused LOUD by design (D-CSUBSET-INLINE-ASM-TEXT).
@code
int dss_asm_volatile_gnu_probe(int x) { __asm__ __volatile__ (""); return x; }
int dss_asm_volatile_gnu_probe2(int x) { __asm__ __volatile (""); return x; }
@end

@probe a_attribute_constructor_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: rejected by DSS, accepted by gcc 13.3.0 and clang 19.1.1. DSS accepts other GNU attributes (packed / aligned / noreturn all pass), so this is a per-ATTRIBUTE hole rather than absent attribute support — which is why it is its own row.
@why GNU `__attribute__((constructor))`.
@code
int dss_attr_constructor_probe(void) __attribute__((constructor));
@end

@probe a_attribute_alias_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: rejected by DSS, accepted by gcc 13.3.0 and clang 19.1.1. Worth noting next to the direction-B extern-realization family: `alias` is one of the reference-compiler mechanisms for STATING that a name resolves to another symbol, i.e. part of the vocabulary DSS invented its own syntax for instead of adopting.
@why GNU `__attribute__((alias("target")))`.
@code
int dss_alias_target_probe(void) { return 1; }
int dss_alias_probe(void) __attribute__((alias("dss_alias_target_probe")));
@end

# ── The two GNU spellings DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED from the `__`-qualifier alias
# cycle (D-CSUBSET-GNU-DUNDER-QUALIFIER-SPELLINGS, 2026-08-12). Neither is a
# spelling alias, so neither could ride that cycle's keyword-row mechanism —
# which is a reason to CENSUS them here, not a reason to leave them invisible.
# The alternative was a silent exclusion, and a gap nobody wrote down is a gap
# the next cycle re-derives from scratch.

@probe a_thread_local_gnu_spelling
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: `extern __thread int x;` is rejected by DSS (P0009 at the declarator — `__thread` is absent from the keyword vocabulary, so it parses as the declared NAME and the following `int` has nowhere to go) and accepted by gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3 and clang 19.1.1 at BOTH `-std=gnu17` and `-std=c17 -pedantic-errors`. Tracked at D-CSUBSET-GNU-DUNDER-QUALIFIER-SPELLINGS (this class's exclusion set). ★ DELIBERATELY NOT CLOSED BY A KEYWORD-ALIAS ROW, and the reason is an OPERATOR DECISION, not an oversight: a plain alias would land `__thread` on `ThreadLocalKeyword` and thereby inherit `_Thread_local`'s POSITION-FREEDOM, shipping a placement rule DSS does not model — and the reference compilers DISAGREE about that rule, so there is no "what gcc/clang do" to copy. ✔MEASURED on `__thread static int x;`: gcc 13.3.0 rejects it as a HARD ERROR at every level (`error: '__thread' before 'static'`), while clang 18.1.3 and clang 19.1.1 ACCEPT it under `-std=gnu17` and reject it only under `-pedantic-errors`, where the same text is a `[-Wpedantic]` warning promoted by `-Werror`. So the two references differ in KIND (error vs pedantic warning), not merely in verdict. A further complication: `_Thread_local` and `thread_local` are DISTINCT kinds in c-subset.lang.json (ThreadLocalKeyword vs ThreadLocalC23Keyword), so `__thread` is not cleanly an alias of either one.
@why GNU `__thread` — the pre-C11 thread-local storage-class specifier, still what glibc's own headers write. ★ THE BODY USES `extern` ON PURPOSE. A DEFINITION (`static __thread int x;`) would ALSO be refused by DSS for a completely unrelated, MEASURED reason — the linker advertises `tbss` on no relocatable-object format (see a_thread_local_definition_c11) — so this row would then red on that gap forever and never turn when the spelling was added. `extern _Thread_local int x;` and `extern thread_local int x;` are both ACCEPTED by DSS today on both fixed targets (✔MEASURED), so with an `extern` body the SPELLING is the only variable left.
@code
extern __thread int dss_thread_local_gnu_probe;
@end

@probe a_extension_prefix_gnu
@direction A
@mode extended
@min-stdc 0
@families gnu,msvc
@expect-ref accept
@acknowledged-gap MEASURED direction-A gap: both positions are rejected by DSS — the DECLARATION prefix at parse (P0009, `__extension__` read as the declared name) and the EXPRESSION prefix in semantics (S0001 "got __extension__", an unknown identifier) — and both are accepted by gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3, clang 19.1.1 at `-std=gnu17` AND `-std=c17 -pedantic-errors`, and by cl 19.51 at `/std:clatest /W4` with ZERO diagnostics (measured with a matched negative control: cl rejects `__no_such_keyword__ int x;` with C2054, so its acceptance here is recognition, not leniency). Tracked at D-CSUBSET-GNU-DUNDER-QUALIFIER-SPELLINGS (this class's exclusion set). ★ DELIBERATELY NOT FOLDED INTO THE SPELLING-ALIAS CYCLE: `__extension__` has NO plain-C counterpart to alias onto. It is a transparent diagnostic-suppression prefix — a small GRAMMAR feature in two distinct slots (declaration-specifier position and unary-expression position) that must parse and then vanish, leaving the operand's type and value untouched. Calling that an alias would have mis-scoped it, and a keyword row cannot express it at all.
@why GNU `__extension__` — the prefix that suppresses pedantic diagnostics for the construct it precedes. It is how glibc and libstdc++ headers keep `-pedantic` builds quiet, so a translation unit reaching those headers meets it without ever writing it.
@code
__extension__ int dss_extension_decl_probe;
int dss_extension_expr_probe(void) { return __extension__ (0); }
@end
